title: Artifact Synthesis

files:

  - .assets/img-103-03-01.png
  - .assets/img-103-03-02.png
  - segger.arm.sandbox:segger.arm.distro.sandbox/BuildC.em.ts
  - segger.arm.sandbox:segger.arm.distro.sandbox/StartupC.em.ts

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Its Unique Position}

      The anchor of every distro, you'll never encounter an **EMS** source file which _explicitly_ references {[cu]BuildC}.

      Why&thinsp;??&ensp; Because the **EMS** translator automatically brings {[cu]BuildC} into the mix when building any program.

      Said another way: &nbsp; all `.em.ts` source files _implicitly_ con&shy;tain an {[ck]import}&hellip;{[ck]from}{[cs]'@$distro/BuildC.em'} directive.

      <div class="em-info">

      {[cu]BuildC} itself contains multiple {[ck]import} directives, each bringing additional units into the **EMS** working set.

      </div>

      Before moving ahead, adjust {[em]Board} and {[em]Setup} to once again select our skeletal `segger.arm.sandbox` distro.

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,playground]&thinsp;A Distro Playground}

      As a rule, _every_&thinsp; **EMS** distro package will contain a {[cu]BuildC} com&shy;posite &ndash; including the skeletal distro illustrated here.

      Stripped to its essentials, `segger.arm.sandbox` provides a "playground" for disecting the **EMS** build flow in isolation.

      <div class="em-info">

      Our <code>..sandbox</code> distro even lacks its own {[cu]BoardC} for configuring a target MCU platform.

      </div>

      You've already seen the target C++ project generated by the `segger.arm.sandbox` distro {[tr,103/02/05]}.

      So let's finally move upstream to {[cu]BuildC} &ndash; as well as ex&shy;amine other source files found in our distro playground.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 2
      - mark 3 B 16
      - mark 3 C 27
    focus: [3,1]
    text: |
      {[ht,eyeglasses_2]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Composite Overview}

      The directive at {[bm,A]}&thinsp; declares {[cu]BuildC} as a **Composite** &ndash; the third of four possible roles an **EMS** unit can assume.

      <div class="em-info">

      We first introduced **Composites** in a early tour &thinsp;{[tr,100/03/09]} using the ever-present {[cu]BoardC} as an exemplar.

      </div>

      Unlike the more prevalent **Module** &thinsp;{[tr,100/03/02]}, a **Composite** _only_ exists in the **meta** domain during program build.

      As such, {[cu]BuildC} can define intrinsic meta-functions like {[cs]em$configure}{[bm,B]} outside of a <code class="cmd-cr">namespace</code>**`em$meta`** block.

      The {[cu]BuildC} composite also features {[cs]em$generate}{[bm,C]} &ndash; an intrinsic meta-function for synthesizing build-time files.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 1 4
      - mark 3 2 6
      - mark 3 3 8
      - mark 3 4 9
      - mark 3 5 10
    focus: [3,1]
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Generating C++ Code}

      Using their own {[cs]em$generate} meta-functions, several units imported by {[cu]BuildC} synthesize C++ source files:

      {[bm,1]}&thinsp; {[cu]ArmStartupC} &rarr; `em.arch.arm/startup.cpp`<br>
      {[bm,2]}&thinsp; {[cu]IntrVec} &rarr; `em.arch.arm/intr.cpp`<br>
      {[bm,3]}&thinsp; {[cu]REGS} &rarr; `segger.arm.distro.sandbox/REGS.hpp`<br>
      {[bm,4]}&thinsp; {[cu]StartupC} &rarr; `..distro.sandbox/startup.cpp`<br>
      {[bm,5]}&thinsp; {[cu]TargC} &rarr; `emscript.hpp`<br>

      Our earlier `main.cpp` program corresponding to {[cu]Gist00} will then (in-)directly {[ck]#include} each of these files.

      <div class="em-info">

      If possible, open up the actual `main.cpp` file illustrated in a prior stop {[tr,103/02/03]} on this tour.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 4
    focus: [4,1]
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;%[cu]StartupC% &ndash; Generating <code>startup.cpp</code>}

      To illustrate {[cs]em$generate} in action, we'll examine how the {[cu]StartupC} composite generates `startup.cpp`.

      Imported by {[cu]BuildC}, **EMS** invokes this {[cu]StartupC} meta-function during its regular program translation process.

      Most {[cs]em$generate} definitions leverage {[cs]$outfile} &ndash; an intrinsic utility only available in the **EMS** meta-domain.

      The object returned by {[cs]$outfile} supports multiple meth&shy;ods for accumulating textual content &ndash; such as {[cf]addFrag}.

      The {[cf]close} method then writes all of this text to the file path originally handed to {[cs]$outfile}.

      <div class="em-info">

      Note that {[cs]$outfile} resolves all output file paths rela&shy;tive to the `workspace/.out` folder.

      </div>
